# Copilot CLI permission flags applied to EVERY agent run, before the agent's own profile.
# One flag per line; blank lines and '#' comments are ignored. The runner refuses to start if any
# profile contains --allow-all-tools, --allow-all, --yolo, --allow-all-paths, --allow-all-urls, or a
# shell rule for an interpreter (bash, sh, pwsh, python, node, …): those let an agent run anything.
#
# Verified behaviour (Copilot CLI): an unapproved tool is denied automatically in non-interactive
# runs; compound commands (&&, ;, |, $(…)) need every part approved; redirections are denied; shell
# rules match the command plus its first subcommand only, so a three-word deny is NOT enforced.
# File tools are confined to the repository; shell readers are not (see the deny list below).

# The one shell command agents may run: the gateway (fixed checks + read-only git views).
--allow-tool=shell({{GATEWAY}}:*)

# Deny every read-capable command explicitly. Copilot AUTO-APPROVES some read-only commands (cat,
# head, tail, grep, sed, sort, cut, uniq, nl, tr, diff, ls, find, wc, stat, which, printenv, git
# status/log/show/diff/branch/remote…) with no allow rule, and its path check misses `~` paths, so
# `cat ~/.zshrc` or `git diff --no-index ~/…` would leak files from the home folder. Deny rules win
# over that auto-approval; the gateway provides the safe equivalents (checks + git views).
# Unix commands:
--deny-tool=shell(git)
# A rule matches a command plus its first subcommand, so `shell(git)` alone does NOT cover
# `git diff …` (verified). Every git subcommand is denied by name; the gateway has the git views.
--deny-tool=shell(git add)
--deny-tool=shell(git am)
--deny-tool=shell(git apply)
--deny-tool=shell(git archive)
--deny-tool=shell(git bisect)
--deny-tool=shell(git blame)
--deny-tool=shell(git branch)
--deny-tool=shell(git bundle)
--deny-tool=shell(git cat-file)
